<meta type=article title="See source: revealing the authored DSL behind a rendered block (#19)">
</meta>

<config show-source=true />

<section | Mermaid with its source disclosed>

With `<config show-source=true />` on, a rendered-from-DSL block emits the
authored source alongside the rendered output, inside a native
`<details><summary>See source</summary><pre>…</pre></details>` control. No
JavaScript: `<details>` is a native HTML toggle. The disclosure's `<pre>` is
plain (no `mermaid` class, no `data-enscribe-dsl`), so a live in-browser scan or
a build-time static render never touches it — the source survives verbatim.

<mermaid #diagram:flow>
graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}
  B -->|yes| C[OK]
  B -->|no| D[Stop]
</mermaid>

<section | ABC with its source disclosed>

The same switch covers the other diagram engine. The disclosure rides the
existing frameable emission — it is the last sibling of the figure block, after
the rendered output and its caption.

<abc #music:twinkle caption="A familiar tune.">
X:1
T:Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star
M:4/4
L:1/4
K:C
C C G G | A A G2 |
</abc>

The control's look is a theme concern: the document says *what* (source is
available, via the `enscribe-source` class hook); the theme says *how* (it can
be styled to look like a button). The baseline here is the unstyled native
control.
